1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:05,380
This video strives to be simply the most accurate
assessment of Boeing’s troubles to fit within

2
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:06,730
twenty minutes.

3
00:00:06,730 --> 00:00:10,500
To achieve this, its script was written in
collaboration with and based on reporting

4
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:15,099
by Jon Ostrower and his team of journalists
at The Air Current, although any conclusions

5
00:00:15,099 --> 00:00:16,099
are our own.

6
00:00:16,099 --> 00:00:19,120
They’re some of the most respected names
in the space and recent weeks have been an

7
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:24,269
extremely busy and opportune time for aviation
journalists, so we are immensely grateful

8
00:00:24,269 --> 00:00:26,560
for their contributions.

9
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:28,010
It wasn’t always this bad. 

10
00:00:28,010 --> 00:00:33,680
Long before a door plug rocked Boeing, and
long before two nosediving 737 MAXs shook

11
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,329
the iconic American aerospace giant to its
very core, it was an earthquake of the most

12
00:00:38,329 --> 00:00:39,680
literal variety. 

13
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:46,410
It was right about here, at 10:55 in the morning,
February 28, 2001, that a magnitude 6.8 quake

14
00:00:46,410 --> 00:00:50,880
sent shockwaves rolling across the Pacific
Northwest; and they didn’t spare the facilities

15
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:54,300
of one of the region’s most prized institutions,
either.

16
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:59,320
For a full minute, it shook nerves and structures
alike across Boeing’s various Washington

17
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:05,260
locations, cracking runways, destabilizing
foundations, and ruining, in particular, this

18
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:11,560
building: 10-85, home to a host of engineers
working on the heavily in-demand 737. 

19
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:17,200
In this case, though, a ground shaking event
didn’t prove a catastrophe, but a catalyst—an

20
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,869
opportunity for Boeing to be its best Boeing.

21
00:01:19,869 --> 00:01:24,509
Rather than move staff to office space farther
afield, or rebuild offices, Boeing Commercial

22
00:01:24,509 --> 00:01:29,049
Airplanes’ leadership boldly innovated—they
moved to the lake. 

23
00:01:29,049 --> 00:01:33,780
The company’s Renton campus has been the
home of 737 assembly since the 1970s.

24
00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:38,770
By the ‘90s, the location had sprawled to
nearly 300-acres, and the facilities had never

25
00:01:38,770 --> 00:01:40,049
been busier.

26
00:01:40,049 --> 00:01:44,590
Trying to keep up with the seemingly insatiable
demand for ever cheaper domestic human haulers,

27
00:01:44,590 --> 00:01:49,360
trying to match the A320’s technological
advances, and trying to streamline construction

28
00:01:49,360 --> 00:01:53,930
and assembly, Boeing launched the 737 Next
Generation in 1993.

29
00:01:53,930 --> 00:02:00,280
On top of the now more efficient, higher capacity,
fully digitally designed 737 NG, Boeing also

30
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:03,280
sought to reinvent how they built planes in
the first place.

31
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:07,780
Visiting Toyota and other Japanese companies
throughout the 1990s, executives sought to

32
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:13,129
simplify the supply chain, adopting the 5S's
of lean methodology and Just in Time principles

33
00:02:13,129 --> 00:02:17,050
as means to catch up on their back orders
and reduce idle inventory.

34
00:02:17,050 --> 00:02:21,660
By 2000, such C-suite ideas had matriculated
down to the factory floor.

35
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:27,140
In Renton, Boeing had swept, simplified, sorted,
and sold off so much unneeded stock and general

36
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:32,080
clutter, that it now had acres of newly unspoken-for
floor space and storage.

37
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:35,069
Rather than expand to meet demand, they shrank.

38
00:02:35,069 --> 00:02:39,550
Then, when the earthquake shook the region,
and then 9/11 shook the world, the company

39
00:02:39,550 --> 00:02:44,379
took advantage of upheaval and slowdown by
moving the entire 737 team—from ground floor

40
00:02:44,379 --> 00:02:48,530
machinists to high-ranking engineers—into
the same building. 

41
00:02:48,530 --> 00:02:52,440
By effectively decluttering, Boeing moved
engineering and business offices into the

42
00:02:52,440 --> 00:02:56,269
three buildings running the floor’s east
and west boundaries and its center.

43
00:02:56,269 --> 00:03:00,640
By closing the distance between machinist,
engineer, and management, difficulties in

44
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:04,520
assembly, pain points, and slowdowns were
trimmed away one by one.

45
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:08,370
By closing the distance between plane and
parts, placing the assembly on a moving line

46
00:03:08,370 --> 00:03:12,490
transporting the plane from one station to
the next at a rate of two inches an hour,

47
00:03:12,490 --> 00:03:17,360
the factory was clearing an output of more
than 30 planes a month by 2010, and eventually

48
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:22,239
halved individual production time from 22
days to just 11.

49
00:03:22,239 --> 00:03:26,810
With the move to the lake complete, nearly
8,000 NGs would take to the sky in the following

50
00:03:26,810 --> 00:03:31,989
twenty years, making this third generation
737 the best selling generation of what was

51
00:03:31,989 --> 00:03:35,830
then the best selling commercial aircraft
in the world. 

52
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:40,299
Producing a safe, reliable product through
a quick, efficient, stable model that provided

53
00:03:40,299 --> 00:03:44,269
ample room for collaboration, communication
from leadership, and continuous improvement

54
00:03:44,269 --> 00:03:49,930
through engineers and on-the-floor machinists,
Boeing by the mid-2000s had reached peak Boeing.

55
00:03:49,930 --> 00:03:54,850
Alongside the successful 777 launch in ‘95—which
is still the most popular wide body plane

56
00:03:54,850 --> 00:03:59,900
the world over, the 737 NG development, the
proactive restructuring of its Renton factory,

57
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:04,360
and the sound maneuvering to maintain an upperhand
in its competition with Airbus, Boeing was

58
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:09,830
increasingly dependable, Boeing was revered,
Boeing was an American institution that Americans

59
00:04:09,830 --> 00:04:11,250
were proud of.

60
00:04:11,250 --> 00:04:16,340
Of course, while reaching a peak is difficult,
so too is maintaining a peak, as the potential

61
00:04:16,340 --> 00:04:19,440
to slip is never too far off. 

62
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:24,940
At about the same time, the company also decided
to take a stab at a bolder project with an

63
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:28,350
even bolder business model not so far up the
road.

64
00:04:28,350 --> 00:04:33,690
36 miles or 58 kilometers north on highway
405 from Boeing’s Renton factory is Boeing's

65
00:04:33,690 --> 00:04:37,320
Everett factory—the single largest building
in the world.

66
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:41,220
And it’s here where its troubles first appeared.

67
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:45,380
Of course, when developing brand new planes
for brand new purposes there’s bound to

68
00:04:45,380 --> 00:04:46,889
be a few swings and misses.

69
00:04:46,889 --> 00:04:52,470
But it’s less the miss and more the style
of the swing that illustrates how far and

70
00:04:52,470 --> 00:04:54,810
how quick Boeing fell.  

71
00:04:54,810 --> 00:04:59,630
In 2003, Boeing decided they were going to
make a new plane—it’d come to be called

72
00:04:59,630 --> 00:05:01,410
the 787 Dreamliner.

73
00:05:01,410 --> 00:05:06,620
But the board and CEO and ultimately investors
were tired of the cycle of spending huge sums

74
00:05:06,620 --> 00:05:11,740
to design a plane then spending years upon
years for them to finally turn a profit—they

75
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:13,740
wanted returns faster.

76
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:18,471
In fact, they said they’d do it for less
than half of what they’d spent on the 777

77
00:05:18,471 --> 00:05:19,471
a decade ago.

78
00:05:19,471 --> 00:05:24,050
And then, to cap off the challenge, they were
going to introduce a litany of new technologies

79
00:05:24,050 --> 00:05:29,729
including, most importantly, the first majority
composite airframe of any commercial aircraft,

80
00:05:29,729 --> 00:05:34,500
ever—a wholesale revolution in how jetliners
had always been built.

81
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:35,669
But Boeing had a solution.

82
00:05:35,669 --> 00:05:40,930
A decade before, McDonnell Douglas was the
last great American commercial aircraft manufacturer

83
00:05:40,930 --> 00:05:46,139
that wasn’t Boeing, yet like all others
in this category eventually did, in the 1990s,

84
00:05:46,139 --> 00:05:50,139
as the Cold War ended and Airbus strengthened,
it was struggling financially.

85
00:05:50,139 --> 00:05:54,530
It needed a new aircraft to have any shot
of survival, yet it didn’t have the cash

86
00:05:54,530 --> 00:05:59,350
to design a new aircraft, so rather it aggregated
a collection of suppliers to take on the design

87
00:05:59,350 --> 00:06:00,350
risk themselves.

88
00:06:00,350 --> 00:06:04,650
They’d each pay to design their respective
parts, McDonnell Douglas would put it all

89
00:06:04,650 --> 00:06:07,970
together, and they’d all profit based on
the sales.

90
00:06:07,970 --> 00:06:12,120
Ultimately unable to turn around its fortunes,
McDonnell Douglas merged with Boeing in 1997

91
00:06:12,120 --> 00:06:19,060
and the new, larger Boeing rebranded the beleaguered
MD-95 the 717 and committed to bringing it

92
00:06:19,060 --> 00:06:20,060
to market.

93
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:25,710
This went… ok—there were some major hiccups
that they ultimately overcame—although it’s

94
00:06:25,710 --> 00:06:30,400
hard to really tell because the aircraft was
a complete flop financially and Boeing preferred

95
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:32,270
to sell the 737.

96
00:06:32,270 --> 00:06:38,340
Only 155 were sold meaning the risk that the
suppliers took on was never truly rewarded. 

97
00:06:38,340 --> 00:06:44,170
Yet also now part of the new, larger Boeing
were all the former McDonnell Douglas executives.

98
00:06:44,170 --> 00:06:48,030
When faced with the challenge of how to make
a new aircraft for less, they felt they already

99
00:06:48,030 --> 00:06:50,900
had the solution: they’d do the 717 strategy.

100
00:06:50,900 --> 00:06:56,210
They’d collect together subcontractors—Japan’s
Heavy Industrial conglomerates, Alenia Aeronautica,

101
00:06:56,210 --> 00:07:00,810
Vought Aircraft Industries—assign them parts,
and not only ask them to produce the parts,

102
00:07:00,810 --> 00:07:02,720
but to design them too.

103
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:06,740
And most crucially, Boeing wouldn’t pay
for the development—at least not nearly

104
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:08,919
to the extent they had previously.

105
00:07:08,919 --> 00:07:12,490
Once again, in order to win the contracts,
the suppliers had to foot the bulk of the

106
00:07:12,490 --> 00:07:16,010
upfront development costs and make it back
long-term through what Boeing and airlines

107
00:07:16,010 --> 00:07:20,199
paid for the parts, spares, and maintenance—or
at least try to. 

108
00:07:20,199 --> 00:07:25,360
Clearly, this was a disadvantageous deal for
the subcontractors, but what the process proved

109
00:07:25,360 --> 00:07:28,470
out was that it did not matter.

110
00:07:28,470 --> 00:07:31,490
Boeing had enormous negotiating leverage.

111
00:07:31,490 --> 00:07:36,030
Holding half of the commercial aircraft market,
any Boeing contract was better than no Boeing

112
00:07:36,030 --> 00:07:41,139
contract so subcontractors agreed and Boeing’s
plan to build its most advanced aircraft in

113
00:07:41,139 --> 00:07:44,740
history at a fraction of previous development
costs marched forward.

114
00:07:44,740 --> 00:07:49,740
The utopian vision to speed up development
and cut the cost of a new airplane had collided

115
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:54,030
with an increasingly transactional approach
of the new Boeing.

116
00:07:54,030 --> 00:07:56,600
This went poorly.

117
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:59,020
The plane was three years delayed.

118
00:07:59,020 --> 00:08:04,520
When rolling the first 787 out to reporters,
dignitaries, and employees on 7/8/7, observers

119
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:08,300
on-hand noticed that if you looked up through
the wheel, you could see daylight from the

120
00:08:08,300 --> 00:08:11,750
cabin, if you looked through the cockpit’s
front windows, you could see the passenger

121
00:08:11,750 --> 00:08:12,750
cabin.

122
00:08:12,750 --> 00:08:17,400
As suppliers and employees alike would later
outline, the plane was less plane and more

123
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:18,440
shell.

124
00:08:18,440 --> 00:08:23,490
Inside it had no floor, no wiring, no systems,
nor plumbing—it was prepared for far-away

125
00:08:23,490 --> 00:08:25,780
photos, but certainly not flight.

126
00:08:25,780 --> 00:08:29,259
And it wouldn’t be for another two and a
half years.

127
00:08:29,259 --> 00:08:33,390
Even once finally flying with airlines, the
expected production cost benefits from the

128
00:08:33,390 --> 00:08:37,839
new technologies never fully came and it had
a rough start to commercial service with a

129
00:08:37,839 --> 00:08:42,010
series of thermal runaway incidents in its
lithium-ion batteries that led to a months-long

130
00:08:42,010 --> 00:08:43,919
grounding in 2013.

131
00:08:43,919 --> 00:08:50,460
The 787, on the surface, has been an unabashed
success for Boeing—2023 saw more than 300

132
00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:55,930
new orders and the delivery of its 1,100th
version—but despite that, it still hasn’t

133
00:08:55,930 --> 00:09:01,610
come close, financially, to delivering on
its original promise for Boeing or its suppliers—ultimately

134
00:09:01,610 --> 00:09:06,630
the inevitable outcome of an overly compartmentalized
development and manufacturing process.

135
00:09:06,630 --> 00:09:08,060
So they didn’t do that again.

136
00:09:08,060 --> 00:09:11,899
Coming out of the global financial crisis,
consumer demand for air travel was beginning

137
00:09:11,899 --> 00:09:16,260
to boom again; and with the fresh memory of
record fuel prices, Airbus had just launched

138
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:21,630
its new generation of fuel-efficient, re-engined
A320 narrowbody aircraft.

139
00:09:21,630 --> 00:09:27,760
Combined, that meant Boeing hardly had a choice—Airbus
was winning once Boeing-exclusive buyers.

140
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:32,310
Boeing had to launch a corresponding fuel-efficient
narrowbody aircraft or risk losing market

141
00:09:32,310 --> 00:09:36,790
share of its most popular product—its core
profit-center—to its competitor.

142
00:09:36,790 --> 00:09:41,560
Yet, Boeing’s top engineers and finances
were still tied up in getting the beleaguered

143
00:09:41,560 --> 00:09:45,940
787 flying, and they certainly were not going
to set up an all-new production system quickly

144
00:09:45,940 --> 00:09:48,630
enough or try the outsourcing strategy again.

145
00:09:48,630 --> 00:09:54,040
That meant that practically they just couldn’t
build a new aircraft—the only way they could

146
00:09:54,040 --> 00:09:57,829
get a fuel-efficient narrowbody to market
soon enough to respond to Airbus with the

147
00:09:57,829 --> 00:10:03,490
resources they had was to make yet another
iteration to their almost five decade-old

148
00:10:03,490 --> 00:10:04,970
737 design.

149
00:10:04,970 --> 00:10:08,370
So, backed into a corner, that’s what they
did. 

150
00:10:08,370 --> 00:10:12,649
Continuing their response to the 787 difficulties,
the company returned to a more traditional

151
00:10:12,649 --> 00:10:14,570
development and manufacturing process.

152
00:10:14,570 --> 00:10:18,910
It’s not that Boeing would do everything,
but rather they’d do more and subcontract

153
00:10:18,910 --> 00:10:23,300
out to a smaller selection of suppliers with
which they could work more closely.

154
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:28,519
Perhaps the most important relationship of
all was with this company: Spirit Aerosystems.

155
00:10:28,519 --> 00:10:33,019
For decades, this Wichita, Kansas factory
had been building Boeing fuselages then shipping

156
00:10:33,019 --> 00:10:36,400
them across the country by rail for final
assembly in Seattle.

157
00:10:36,400 --> 00:10:41,880
And there’s a good reason Spirit was entrusted
to build the literal core of Boeing aircraft—they

158
00:10:41,880 --> 00:10:43,600
used to be Boeing.

159
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:48,550
The company’s Wichita division was an integral
part, building some of their most iconic aircraft

160
00:10:48,550 --> 00:10:51,329
like the B-29, B-47, and B-52.

161
00:10:51,329 --> 00:10:56,850
But in the early 2000s, Boeing needed money
in order to simultaneously build the 787,

162
00:10:56,850 --> 00:11:02,310
appease investors, and appease its new management,
so divestiture was the strategy: they’d

163
00:11:02,310 --> 00:11:08,060
sold plants in St Louis and Spokane and Corinth
and Irving and now it was Wichita and Tulsa’s

164
00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:09,060
turn.

165
00:11:09,060 --> 00:11:14,220
Upon their sale, Boeing and its shareholders
cashed a $1.2 billion check and Spirit Aerosystems

166
00:11:14,220 --> 00:11:19,390
was born to do more or less what they always
had, but now as an independent, autonomous

167
00:11:19,390 --> 00:11:20,390
company.

168
00:11:20,390 --> 00:11:22,900
This went poorly. 

169
00:11:22,900 --> 00:11:27,160
Again in response to the 787’s incredible
cost overruns, Boeing launched the rather

170
00:11:27,160 --> 00:11:30,280
anachronistically named “Partnering for
Success” program.

171
00:11:30,280 --> 00:11:34,019
The way Boeing would describe this was that
the company was going from supplier to supplier

172
00:11:34,019 --> 00:11:38,709
to look for opportunities to ultimately create
a stronger aircraft product for their end-customer.

173
00:11:38,709 --> 00:11:43,209
The way that suppliers would describe this
was that they were getting screwed.

174
00:11:43,209 --> 00:11:47,920
Boeing would go to each—say, UTC, who made
the 777 landing gear—and demand to pay about

175
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:50,320
15% less for the same part.

176
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:54,709
If the supplier didn’t agree, they’d lose
the contract: when UTC refused, the landing

177
00:11:54,709 --> 00:11:59,110
gear contract went to Héroux-Devtek—a comparatively
obscure manufacturer for which this would

178
00:11:59,110 --> 00:12:00,500
be a major coup. 

179
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:04,300
These were suppliers that had demonstrated
their commitment to Boeing by paying to develop

180
00:12:04,300 --> 00:12:08,940
parts of an aircraft which was not theirs
out of pure belief that Boeing would do the

181
00:12:08,940 --> 00:12:13,399
right thing for their mutual success who were
now being told that they themselves weren’t

182
00:12:13,399 --> 00:12:17,800
doing enough for their mutual success and
had to earn less.

183
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:22,350
But no matter how Boeing might portray it,
the dynamics in these relationships were hardly

184
00:12:22,350 --> 00:12:26,649
mutual—to the suppliers, Boeing was irreplaceable.

185
00:12:26,649 --> 00:12:31,699
As an aerospace supplier, there’s Boeing,
there’s Airbus, there’s Embraer, and that’s

186
00:12:31,699 --> 00:12:35,180
it for major commercial aircraft component
customers.

187
00:12:35,180 --> 00:12:40,540
If they want business, they have to work with
Boeing—the imbalance of power is massive—so,

188
00:12:40,540 --> 00:12:45,970
rationally yet unfortunately, plenty agreed
to those price cuts in exchange for yet another

189
00:12:45,970 --> 00:12:50,240
promise that it’ll lead to greater mutual
success thanks to a higher volume of orders

190
00:12:50,240 --> 00:12:51,870
down the line. 

191
00:12:51,870 --> 00:12:55,750
Boeing might’ve hoped that a bit of healthy
supplier squeeze would lead to optimization

192
00:12:55,750 --> 00:13:02,269
and innovation, yet what at very least happened
in tandem was a systemic increase in disruptive

193
00:13:02,269 --> 00:13:03,800
manufacturing issues.

194
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:08,410
After all, the mutual success that Boeing
promised never really materialized.

195
00:13:08,410 --> 00:13:14,190
Boeing’s decision to iterate upon the 737
design, rather than build something new, proved

196
00:13:14,190 --> 00:13:15,350
catastrophic.

197
00:13:15,350 --> 00:13:19,410
The larger size of the more fuel-efficient
engines on the MAX led to slightly different

198
00:13:19,410 --> 00:13:23,350
flight characteristics that likely would have
necessitated greater, and costlier, training

199
00:13:23,350 --> 00:13:29,250
requirements to get a pilot certified on previous
generations of the 737 able to fly the MAX.

200
00:13:29,250 --> 00:13:33,459
So, Boeing implemented a software fix called
MCAS that essentially augmented the inputs

201
00:13:33,459 --> 00:13:37,899
of the pilots so that the aircraft would handle
similarly to previous generations even when,

202
00:13:37,899 --> 00:13:39,519
without the software, it wouldn’t.

203
00:13:39,519 --> 00:13:45,329
It was a flaw in this system, tied to a single
sensor, that was the key trigger in the fatal

204
00:13:45,329 --> 00:13:52,110
crashes of Lion Air Flight 610 in 2018 and
Ethiopian Airlines Flight 302 in 2019, which

205
00:13:52,110 --> 00:13:56,370
in combination led to the 20-month grounding
of the aircraft type.

206
00:13:56,370 --> 00:14:02,350
This led to the loss of hundreds upon hundreds
of orders, nearly $20 billion, but perhaps

207
00:14:02,350 --> 00:14:08,459
more crucially, it meant that Boeing completely
shut down MAX production for months in 2020,

208
00:14:08,459 --> 00:14:13,430
then restarted production at a much-slowed
rate in the middle of the COVID pandemic.

209
00:14:13,430 --> 00:14:17,959
Still today, MAX production has not recovered
to what it was pre-shutdown.

210
00:14:17,959 --> 00:14:23,170
This has been brutal for suppliers—they
had to make massive layoffs to conserve cash,

211
00:14:23,170 --> 00:14:27,810
they lost plenty of their most experienced
workers, and their market caps have lost quite

212
00:14:27,810 --> 00:14:29,500
notable chunks. 

213
00:14:29,500 --> 00:14:35,180
And today, it’s no surprise that suppliers
like Spirit are a source of many of Boeing’s

214
00:14:35,180 --> 00:14:38,730
woes—the two companies have fought bitterly.

215
00:14:38,730 --> 00:14:44,720
Spirit Aerosystems, by itself, has been responsible
for defect after defect that slipped past

216
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:45,720
QA.

217
00:14:45,720 --> 00:14:49,779
The relationship between Boeing and its former
Kansas division has only just begun to right

218
00:14:49,779 --> 00:14:54,350
itself under new management and a new Boeing
contract that lets Spirit finally earn money

219
00:14:54,350 --> 00:15:00,519
on its contribution on the 787 after losing
more than $1 million on each one since 2007.

220
00:15:00,519 --> 00:15:06,029
Altogether, there’s this sequence and sum
of issues occurring across an array of companies

221
00:15:06,029 --> 00:15:10,779
with little commonality between them—some
are strategic failures, some are software

222
00:15:10,779 --> 00:15:15,870
bugs, some are production issues, some are
an unresilient response to an outside force.

223
00:15:15,870 --> 00:15:20,589
Perhaps the only commonality is that they’re
all occurring as a part of this incredible,

224
00:15:20,589 --> 00:15:25,180
global, multi-company machine that builds
a Boeing aircraft.

225
00:15:25,180 --> 00:15:30,040
That leads to one conclusion—the machine
that builds the machine it's broken.

226
00:15:30,040 --> 00:15:35,350
It’s not the suppliers or the airlines or
the pilots or the maintenance engineers, it's

227
00:15:35,350 --> 00:15:36,600
Boeing that’s broken.

228
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:42,470
They are now unable to reliably convert the
singular engineering marvel into a consistent

229
00:15:42,470 --> 00:15:43,670
product.

230
00:15:43,670 --> 00:15:46,279
Yet still, that leaves the question of why. 

231
00:15:46,279 --> 00:15:51,529
These days, commercial aircraft are more or
less a commodity.

232
00:15:51,529 --> 00:15:56,699
The 737 is more or less the same thing as
an a320, the 787 is more or less the same

233
00:15:56,699 --> 00:16:01,839
thing as an a350—there are some differences,
but ultimately orders come down to who’s

234
00:16:01,839 --> 00:16:05,399
offering more or less the same thing quicker
or for less.

235
00:16:05,399 --> 00:16:09,449
The two major aircraft manufacturers have
responded to these commodity economics in

236
00:16:09,449 --> 00:16:11,040
slightly different ways.

237
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:15,569
Both have focused intensively in their factories
and Airbus itself had its own massive and

238
00:16:15,569 --> 00:16:20,180
costly blunder with the A380, but Airbus never
pursued such an extractive strategy with its

239
00:16:20,180 --> 00:16:24,720
suppliers nor did it fight costly cultural
battles with its own workforce.

240
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:29,490
It not only talked about its desire for supply-chain
stability, it actually sought it out by bringing

241
00:16:29,490 --> 00:16:32,459
back parts of the process under its own roof.

242
00:16:32,459 --> 00:16:37,820
That stability has enabled Airbus to push
the younger A320 bit by bit into the best-selling

243
00:16:37,820 --> 00:16:40,230
airplane of all time. 

244
00:16:40,230 --> 00:16:43,720
In the world’s most entrenched duopoly,
Boeing and Airbus know that they’re going

245
00:16:43,720 --> 00:16:47,930
to get orders regardless, since it’s a commodity
market, and so ultimately their financial

246
00:16:47,930 --> 00:16:51,940
success is going to be about the combination
of how fast they can build aircraft and how

247
00:16:51,940 --> 00:16:53,790
cheap they can build aircraft.

248
00:16:53,790 --> 00:16:57,949
Essentially, Boeing is just wholeheartedly
embracing the realities of the market while

249
00:16:57,949 --> 00:17:02,410
fixating on the financial returns of free
cash flows for investors. 

250
00:17:02,410 --> 00:17:05,540
This isn’t working.

251
00:17:05,540 --> 00:17:08,130
Manufacturing is hard.

252
00:17:08,130 --> 00:17:11,150
Manufacturing an aircraft is harder.

253
00:17:11,150 --> 00:17:14,740
Manufacturing an aircraft profitably is even
harder.

254
00:17:14,740 --> 00:17:17,890
Manufacturing an aircraft capable of flying
the majority of every single day for two or

255
00:17:17,890 --> 00:17:21,620
three decades designed to be safer than walking
down the street in conditions ranging from

256
00:17:21,620 --> 00:17:26,390
forty below to 120 above during anything from
a clear, sunny day to a hurricane profitably

257
00:17:26,390 --> 00:17:30,380
while simultaneously engaged in a perpetual
chase of even greater profits through an endless

258
00:17:30,380 --> 00:17:33,990
fight between its constituent parts has perhaps
proven too hard. 

259
00:17:33,990 --> 00:17:39,020
It’s a process so starkly removed from,
say, manufacturing a toaster that it should

260
00:17:39,020 --> 00:17:41,419
hardly be thought of as a similar process.

261
00:17:41,419 --> 00:17:45,410
It’s perhaps the difference between assembling
a hamburger and assembling an entree in a

262
00:17:45,410 --> 00:17:47,330
Michelin-starred kitchen.

263
00:17:47,330 --> 00:17:52,450
Assembling a hamburger can be systematized,
segmented, optimized, and outsourced—there

264
00:17:52,450 --> 00:17:57,020
are plenty of ways that an experienced business-person
can come into that process and find corners

265
00:17:57,020 --> 00:18:01,520
to cut yet still successfully create good
products and better financial results, then

266
00:18:01,520 --> 00:18:04,919
scale and scale it to achieve yet greater
financial returns. 

267
00:18:04,919 --> 00:18:07,460
But you don’t see Michelin-starred franchises.

268
00:18:07,460 --> 00:18:11,659
That’s because you don’t get the best
food in the world by telling your cooks how

269
00:18:11,659 --> 00:18:13,419
to make the best food in the world.

270
00:18:13,419 --> 00:18:17,840
It is not something that can be systematized,
segmented, optimized, or outsourced.

271
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:21,559
You don’t just hand them a recipe and hope
for the best.

272
00:18:21,559 --> 00:18:26,159
You get the best food in the world through
the sum of the small things: the best ingredients

273
00:18:26,159 --> 00:18:29,850
possible, the cleanest working environment,
the best-designed kitchen, pay that attracts

274
00:18:29,850 --> 00:18:34,059
the best talent, a sustainable working schedule,
mentorship between older and newer staff,

275
00:18:34,059 --> 00:18:39,049
and on and on and on into smaller and smaller
minutia to chase margins of quality through

276
00:18:39,049 --> 00:18:44,400
people, not processes, and only then, when
they’ve been given every opportunity to

277
00:18:44,400 --> 00:18:48,280
succeed, holding them to the absolute highest
standards.

278
00:18:48,280 --> 00:18:52,970
Boeing is trying to be a Michelin-starred
kitchen with a fast-food mindset, and it’s

279
00:18:52,970 --> 00:18:54,970
just not working.

280
00:18:54,970 --> 00:19:00,130
Boeing was that Michelin-starred kitchen—there
was an era when they had an incredible, almost

281
00:19:00,130 --> 00:19:05,929
otherworldly ability to shepherd an entire
industry and the world into a new era of modernity

282
00:19:05,929 --> 00:19:08,809
through the awe-inspiring machines it built.

283
00:19:08,809 --> 00:19:15,059
Yet as the days of today's bleaker era accumulate,
its ability to return to the old one grows

284
00:19:15,059 --> 00:19:16,110
weaker.

285
00:19:16,110 --> 00:19:21,591
Boeing’s best people are leaving—they’re
retiring or growing tired of working for a

286
00:19:21,591 --> 00:19:26,110
company that stumbles, only to promise reform
just before the next stumble.

287
00:19:26,110 --> 00:19:30,240
The company needs a new aircraft, yet the
people with the knowhow from building the

288
00:19:30,240 --> 00:19:33,530
last one are growing scarce.

289
00:19:33,530 --> 00:19:35,679
Consequences are mounting.

290
00:19:35,679 --> 00:19:39,809
Cost-consciousness led to cost overruns that
led to further cost-consciousness and so on

291
00:19:39,809 --> 00:19:45,710
and so forth, so the very thing that the company
focuses on most is where it’s failing most.

292
00:19:45,710 --> 00:19:49,940
The string of two-decades of issues has meant
that, while Boeing is still plenty capable

293
00:19:49,940 --> 00:19:54,830
of building overwhelmingly-safe aircraft and
attracting plenty of orders, its ability to

294
00:19:54,830 --> 00:19:59,210
actually financially succeed while selling
the commodity at the price the market demands

295
00:19:59,210 --> 00:20:00,760
is waning.

296
00:20:00,760 --> 00:20:05,429
With that, they have less cash and more debt
getting in the way of building that new aircraft

297
00:20:05,429 --> 00:20:10,070
they so desperately need, meaning they focus
more on optimizing their existing programs,

298
00:20:10,070 --> 00:20:14,830
meaning more cost-consciousness, and the cycle
continues. 

299
00:20:14,830 --> 00:20:18,429
Boeing is at the bottom of a deep, dark hole.

300
00:20:18,429 --> 00:20:20,049
Their competitor is not.

301
00:20:20,049 --> 00:20:24,090
There’s not some crucial difference between
Boeing and Airbus, there’s not one quick

302
00:20:24,090 --> 00:20:29,330
fix the American manufacturer can make to
reignite the fire, there’s just a fundamental

303
00:20:29,330 --> 00:20:33,290
way of doing things that simply is not working.

304
00:20:33,290 --> 00:20:38,650
That means the only way out of this hole is
the long, boring, awful process that does

305
00:20:38,650 --> 00:20:45,380
not conclude a video about it well of just
being better, in every way, on every day,

306
00:20:45,380 --> 00:20:48,170
from its leadership down to the factory floor.

307
00:20:48,170 --> 00:20:52,830
In an industry where a few missing bolts can
blow open a door plug, ground a fleet, and

308
00:20:52,830 --> 00:20:57,990
tank a stock price, it’s the small things
that matter, yet Boeing's big issue is its

309
00:20:57,990 --> 00:21:04,250
misguided belief that it can cut and squeeze
those it needs most without it all falling

310
00:21:04,250 --> 00:21:05,770
apart.

311
00:21:05,770 --> 00:21:11,790
While Boeing is the US’ only commercial
aircraft manufacturer, there are other aircraft

312
00:21:11,790 --> 00:21:17,220
manufacturers—in fact, there’s another
aerospace company just as big as Boeing: Lockheed

313
